1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,360
course of southern agriculture

2
00:00:03,361 --> 00:00:06,640
he turned soybeans into plastic

3
00:00:06,641 --> 00:00:09,720
and carved his place in history

4
00:00:09,721 --> 00:00:11,720
an orphan slave set free

5
00:00:11,721 --> 00:00:15,400
with a microscope and a vision

6
00:00:15,401 --> 00:00:17,340
George Washington Carver Tech

7
00:00:17,341 --> 00:00:22,340
on Modern Marvels

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,820
What do biodiesel fuel and peanut butter have in common?

9
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:38,320
How are crayons and space travel connected?

10
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:42,460
What are soybeans doing in our automobiles?

11
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:48,880
These marvels can all be traced back to the inventive mind of a man who was born a slave,

12
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:52,640
but emerged a master of scientific discovery.

13
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:57,520
He was able to just look around him and see the raw materials,

14
00:00:57,521 --> 00:01:02,320
materials and then see the human need that was out there and bring the two together.

15
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:07,200
And so my own personal view of Carver is that he was a master integrator.

16
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:11,460
Carver had the most remarkable heart.

17
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:14,120
And that's the thing I think I love the most about his life.

18
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:20,380
I mean, as tremendous of a scientist as he was, coming out with so many useful products

19
00:01:20,381 --> 00:01:23,280
that even today, I don't even know what we would do without.

20
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:25,400
His heart was the best.

21
00:01:25,401 --> 00:01:26,101
Dr.

22
00:01:26,102 --> 00:01:31,400
George Washington Carver is well known throughout the world for his revolutionary

23
00:01:31,401 --> 00:01:34,400
research, particularly with a peanut.

24
00:01:34,401 --> 00:01:42,400
In the early 1900s, he discovered and developed more than 300 new uses for the legume, in

25
00:01:42,401 --> 00:01:48,900
food products and industrial applications, from face cream to a cure for dandruff, from

26
00:01:48,901 --> 00:01:52,400
linoleum flooring to motor oil.

27
00:01:52,401 --> 00:01:55,060
Carver saw infinite possibilities in the peanut plant.

28
00:01:55,920 --> 00:02:01,300
He literally inspired other researchers all across this country

29
00:02:01,301 --> 00:02:04,180
to take a deeper look at this plant.

30
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:09,040
And of course, I believe that the end of the line

31
00:02:09,041 --> 00:02:12,100
has not been reached on what he uncovered.

32
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:16,260
But Carver's legacy goes far beyond his accomplishments

33
00:02:16,261 --> 00:02:19,100
with what was called at the time, the lowly goober.

34
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:24,900
He toiled for more than 40 years in his laboratory at Tuskegee Institute in Alabama

35
00:02:24,901 --> 00:02:29,600
and is credited with countless breakthroughs in agricultural research.

36
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:34,180
The impact of that was just astonishing.

37
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:37,900
Carver, through the kind of research he did,

38
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:43,720
was able to really lead the revitalization, really, of a whole region, the southern region.

39
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:47,060
He really is one of the great American heroes.

40
00:02:47,061 --> 00:02:51,780
If there's anyone you're going to learn about, you should learn about, Carver would be one of those people.

41
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:58,920
George Carver was born into slavery in 1864 in Diamond Grove, Missouri.

42
00:03:00,780 --> 00:03:03,400
It was the property of Moses Carver.

43
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:09,340
Towards the end of the Civil War, southern bushwhackers raided the Carver's farm

44
00:03:09,341 --> 00:03:12,120
and kidnapped baby George and his mother.

45
00:03:12,121 --> 00:03:19,760
Moses Carver hired a Union Scout named John Bentley to trace after them and try to recover his property.

46
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:26,160
Bentley was able to find baby George and the mother Mary was never seen again.

47
00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:28,020
We do not know what happened.

48
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:31,080
George was near death when he was found.

49
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:34,720
Moses and his wife took care of him like he was their own child.

50
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,160
Too frail to work in the fields, he helped out with the household chores.

51
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:51,300
It was on this farm that he had spare time to go out and wander in the woods and enjoy

52
00:03:51,301 --> 00:03:56,200
nature and become very close to nature.

53
00:03:56,201 --> 00:04:01,840
He would look at a briar, he would look at the soil, he would look at the different leaf

54
00:04:01,841 --> 00:04:07,000
patterns and question and question and wonder and ask why?

55
00:04:07,001 --> 00:04:09,560
Why are things like this?

56
00:04:09,561 --> 00:04:11,940
George was inspired to paint.

57
00:04:11,941 --> 00:04:15,900
visiting a neighbor's home and seeing works of art for the first time.

58
00:04:15,901 --> 00:04:22,180
So he came back, created his own brushes with twigs and he would take nature's berries and

59
00:04:22,181 --> 00:04:29,760
grasses and create colors, an array of colors, and begin to paint.

60
00:04:29,761 --> 00:04:33,840
Young George Carver's inventiveness and hunger for learning compelled him to seek what was

61
00:04:33,841 --> 00:04:39,780
impossible for many African Americans of his day, a formal education.

62
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:48,740
Around the age of 11, he left the Carver Farm and walked 10 miles to Neosho, Missouri,

63
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:52,520
determined to attend classes at the Lincoln School for Negro Children.

64
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:01,780
He worked for his room and board at the home of Mariah and Andrew Watkins,

65
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:03,940
who lived next door to the school.

66
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:10,240
The story is that at lunch hour, he would jump the fence between the school and Aunt Mariah's house

67
00:05:10,241 --> 00:05:12,080
and go over and help her with her laundry.

68
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:17,660
Many years later, George was inspired to draw this picture of the school

69
00:05:17,661 --> 00:05:19,160
and the Watkins house.

70
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:24,080
George studied hard and got his high school diploma.

71
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:27,660
He applied to Highland College in Kansas and was accepted.

72
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:29,300
It was a big moment.

73
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:33,740
Then he went to Highland and he showed up and they said,

74
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:35,040
we didn't know you were black.

75
00:05:35,580 --> 00:05:36,760
And they turned him away.

76
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:40,140
It was a huge disappointment.

77
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:41,731
George didn't give up.

78
00:05:41,732 --> 00:05:47,560
In his mid-twenties, he applied to Simpson College in Iowa, where blacks were allowed.

79
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:51,620
He was accepted and began taking classes in painting.

80
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:56,920
His first love was art, and he had a teacher whose name was Etta Budd.

81
00:05:57,380 --> 00:06:01,840
She was very dubious about whether a black could make it in art.

82
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:07,620
It was a near impossibility, and she was a little concerned about him being...

83
00:06:07,621 --> 00:06:12,280
he was not the first black at Simpson, but he was the only one at the time.

84
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:17,940
Carver's complex understanding of nature came through in his paintings and drawings at Simpson.

85
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:24,960
Etta Bud noticed this talent and encouraged him to change his course of study to horticulture.

86
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:32,400
Carver took her advice and transferred to the Iowa State College of Agriculture in Ames,

87
00:06:32,620 --> 00:06:34,900
where he became the first black student.

88
00:06:34,901 --> 00:06:40,060
and he had some trouble with dining room privileges and other things.

89
00:06:40,940 --> 00:06:44,180
But he overcame them by his knowledge,

90
00:06:44,181 --> 00:06:49,020
which was so conspicuously immense

91
00:06:49,021 --> 00:06:52,340
that you didn't have to be in his presence very long

92
00:06:52,341 --> 00:06:56,700
to realize that this man knew things.

93
00:06:57,540 --> 00:07:00,040
When Carver completed his bachelor's degree,

94
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:03,020
Iowa state officials offered him a teaching position.

95
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:10,220
The first black student in the college's history was now its first African-American professor.

96
00:07:11,580 --> 00:07:19,720
Though Carver was content at Iowa State, in April 1896, he was offered an opportunity that would change his life forever.

97
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:27,880
Booker T. Washington, a leader among the newly freed slave population in the South,

98
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:32,700
invited him to come to Tuskegee Institute, an all-black college in Alabama.

99
00:07:32,701 --> 00:07:41,613
When Booker T became aware of Carver that there was an agricultural expert in the country anywhere who was black, that was huge.

100
00:07:41,614 --> 00:07:44,955
He was really the only one in the whole country.

101
00:07:44,956 --> 00:07:47,740
Booker T badly wanted someone like that.

102
00:07:47,741 --> 00:07:53,800
At the age of 31, George Washington Carver had found his true calling.

103
00:07:54,860 --> 00:08:01,640
He went to Alabama with a mission, to help a once enslaved population find its way to self-sufficiency.

104
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:06,320
Little did he know what landmark discoveries lay ahead.

105
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:13,400
One of George Washington Carver's closest friends at Iowa State was James Wilson,

106
00:08:13,401 --> 00:08:18,160
would later become Secretary of Agriculture under President William McKinley.

107
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:23,560
George Washington Carver Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

108
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:31,220
We now return to George Washington Carver Tech on Modern Marvels.

109
00:08:34,020 --> 00:08:38,020
Today, 50,000 peanut farms thrive in the United States,

110
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:40,240
most of them in the South.

111
00:08:40,241 --> 00:08:44,800
virtually all of them have taken root in the past century

112
00:08:44,801 --> 00:08:49,560
thanks to the horticulture professor who ventured to Tuskegee

113
00:08:49,561 --> 00:08:52,560
with a sense of mission in 1896

114
00:08:52,561 --> 00:08:58,280
when George Washington Carver arrived by rail in Alabama

115
00:08:58,281 --> 00:09:05,480
he saw no peanut fields instead he was dismayed to see only one crop growing in

116
00:09:05,481 --> 00:09:06,920
the dry sandy soil

117
00:09:06,921 --> 00:09:13,920
In 1794, Eli Whitney invented the cotton gin.

118
00:09:13,921 --> 00:09:18,920
And from that date on into 1900, cotton was king.

119
00:09:18,921 --> 00:09:22,920
Carver knew that a single crop system was not good for the soil.

120
00:09:22,921 --> 00:09:29,920
Any time in agriculture you base your economy on one single crop, you're vulnerable.

121
00:09:29,921 --> 00:09:37,560
In a fertility standpoint, you're depleting the soil and maybe not returning all the nutrients there that is taken.

122
00:09:38,340 --> 00:09:43,080
Carver also recognized that the only thing more vulnerable than the nutrient deficient soil

123
00:09:43,081 --> 00:09:50,020
was the multitude of poor black sharecroppers working in the cotton fields from sunrise to sunset.

124
00:09:52,120 --> 00:09:58,560
They were tied into the industrial system in the northeast where they had the mills, cotton mills,

125
00:09:58,561 --> 00:10:03,560
It was a big system and they were all captive in it.

126
00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:10,560
Carver was anxious to get to work to solve the South's agricultural dilemma.

127
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:17,560
But when he arrived at Tuskegee Institute, he discovered that he didn't even have a laboratory in which to work.

128
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:22,560
And Tuskegee had no money to properly establish one.

129
00:10:22,561 --> 00:10:32,700
So he went out into a local junkyard and found old pots and pans and spoons and took his students and said, we are now collecting the implements for our laboratory.

130
00:10:33,540 --> 00:10:37,660
He was the absolute first person to bring science to Tuskegee University.

131
00:10:38,380 --> 00:10:42,100
He was one of those great visionaries that, you know, when Booker T.

132
00:10:42,101 --> 00:10:46,860
Washington shows him this campus and there is no agricultural college, there's nothing.

133
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:51,400
You know, he had the ability to take nothing and make it into something.

134
00:10:51,401 --> 00:10:54,920
and I think that his life was a great example of that.

135
00:10:56,880 --> 00:10:59,560
Carver started reaching out to the farmers in the area,

136
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:02,720
showing them how the cotton they grew year after year

137
00:11:02,721 --> 00:11:05,680
was sapping the nitrogen and other nutrients from the soil.

138
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:11,020
He encouraged them to plant crops that would restore these important elements back into the land,

139
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:15,380
crops like sweet potatoes, cowpeas, soybeans, and peanuts.

140
00:11:15,880 --> 00:11:20,820
He was another one who came here because he cared about the man furthest down.

141
00:11:20,821 --> 00:11:22,180
He cared about the poor.

142
00:11:22,181 --> 00:11:26,260
He cared about African American people who were poor in the rural areas.

143
00:11:26,840 --> 00:11:29,860
And as such, he took his science to the people.

144
00:11:31,560 --> 00:11:38,220
Carver designed and implemented what he called a movable school to educate the farmers about crop rotation.

145
00:11:39,620 --> 00:11:43,360
Many took his message to heart, but few acted on his advice.

146
00:11:44,180 --> 00:11:55,300
The farmers agreed, but they did not immediately heed because they could not get loans at the bank for any crop except cotton.

147
00:11:55,580 --> 00:11:59,440
Yeah, it was the cash crop at the time, and a lot of people, that was their source of income,

148
00:11:59,720 --> 00:12:05,400
and it would have been very difficult for those farmers, especially the poorer farmers, to change over from cotton to peanuts.

149
00:12:06,020 --> 00:12:08,060
And there was no market at the time for peanuts.

150
00:12:08,061 --> 00:12:14,420
Carver's concern for southern agriculture wasn't restricted to how cotton was bankrupting the soil.

151
00:12:16,460 --> 00:12:19,220
Another threat was lurking menacingly on the horizon.

152
00:12:20,680 --> 00:12:25,400
The boll weevil, the dreaded insect that had already decimated the cotton crops in Texas,

153
00:12:26,080 --> 00:12:27,920
was making its way across the south.

154
00:12:29,880 --> 00:12:34,620
And it was moving through Mississippi, Louisiana, and headed to Alabama and Georgia

155
00:12:34,621 --> 00:12:37,360
at the rate of about 100 miles per year.

156
00:12:37,361 --> 00:12:38,800
and he warned them.

157
00:12:40,120 --> 00:12:42,980
There was no stopping this devastating force of nature.

158
00:12:44,100 --> 00:12:47,820
The weevil actually lays its eggs inside the unripe cotton bowl

159
00:12:47,821 --> 00:12:50,280
and the newborns eat their way out,

160
00:12:50,560 --> 00:12:52,080
destroying the crop in the process.

161
00:12:54,680 --> 00:12:57,560
Carver believed the South's best defense against the bull weevil

162
00:12:57,561 --> 00:12:58,940
was the peanut,

163
00:12:59,620 --> 00:13:01,380
but he had to convince Southern farmers

164
00:13:01,381 --> 00:13:03,140
that peanuts could become the cash crop

165
00:13:03,141 --> 00:13:05,400
that King Cotton had been for so long.

166
00:13:05,401 --> 00:13:15,700
George Washington Carver really became an evangelist for peanut growing in the southeast part of the United States.

167
00:13:16,700 --> 00:13:22,300
In 1902, Carver entered his lab at Tuskegee to begin intense research into the peanut.

168
00:13:24,180 --> 00:13:29,500
He explored its properties and looked for ways to put the protein and vitamin-rich crop to use.

169
00:13:29,501 --> 00:13:40,560
peanuts are made up of oils resins fats sugars and starches and carver came up with more than

170
00:13:40,561 --> 00:13:46,780
300 new uses for them in recipes and in a variety of synthetic applications

171
00:13:46,781 --> 00:13:56,620
and he made paper and ink and shoe polish and floor wax and axle grease and he did have

172
00:13:56,621 --> 00:13:59,320
some kind of a peanut plastic that he'd made.

173
00:13:59,740 --> 00:14:02,400
And all of these uses, 300 of them,

174
00:14:03,540 --> 00:14:09,340
all the way from their soups to soap and shaving cream,

175
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:13,900
he built a foundation block upon which peanuts were ushered in.

176
00:14:15,880 --> 00:14:21,360
As Carver predicted, in 1915, the boll weevil hit the southeastern farms

177
00:14:21,361 --> 00:14:23,780
and completely destroyed the cotton fields.

178
00:14:23,781 --> 00:14:29,200
none of the farmers from Georgia or Alabama could pay off the loans on their land.

179
00:14:31,540 --> 00:14:33,840
But thanks to Carver's zealous advocacy,

180
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:38,400
peanuts were poised to become a viable commercial alternative to cotton.

181
00:14:39,980 --> 00:14:45,300
By 1917, cotton was dethroned and peanuts was lifted up

182
00:14:45,301 --> 00:14:49,960
and the acres increased in the southeast 400% in just two years.

183
00:14:49,961 --> 00:14:57,600
As peanut power spread across the South, Carver continued to experiment with a now lucrative

184
00:14:57,601 --> 00:14:58,600
crop.

185
00:14:58,601 --> 00:15:04,980
While analyzing the properties of peanut oil, he discovered that it was very easily absorbed

186
00:15:04,981 --> 00:15:10,240
when rubbed onto the skin.

187
00:15:10,241 --> 00:15:14,460
Carver promoted peanut oil as an effective lubricant to be used in vigorous massage treatments

188
00:15:14,461 --> 00:15:21,460
for those suffering from muscular disorders.

189
00:15:22,620 --> 00:15:27,380
Thousands of patients, including those crippled by polio, came to Tuskegee to receive peanut

190
00:15:27,381 --> 00:15:31,680
oil massages.

191
00:15:31,681 --> 00:15:35,340
Even Moore wrote to Carver to purchase the oil.

192
00:15:35,341 --> 00:15:38,460
While it was never considered a cure for any disease,

193
00:15:38,461 --> 00:15:44,340
those who underwent the treatment reported encouraging results.

194
00:15:44,341 --> 00:15:47,340
word of carver's promising work made it all the way

195
00:15:47,341 --> 00:15:49,800
to the white house

196
00:15:49,801 --> 00:15:52,440
one of the extractions that he did with the peanut

197
00:15:52,441 --> 00:15:54,940
uh... one of our presidents roosevelt

198
00:15:54,941 --> 00:15:59,940
used it for his infantile paralysis

199
00:15:59,941 --> 00:16:02,080
president franklin delano roosevelt

200
00:16:02,081 --> 00:16:06,060
paid an official visit to tuskegee

201
00:16:06,061 --> 00:16:08,340
he greeted carver in person

202
00:16:08,341 --> 00:16:13,860
and applauded the research being done on the alabama campus

203
00:16:13,861 --> 00:16:19,340
I'm proud to come to Tuskegee because I'm proud of what Tuskegee has done.

204
00:16:22,300 --> 00:16:25,320
While Carver has been called the father of the peanut industry,

205
00:16:25,940 --> 00:16:28,840
there is one product that many mistakenly attribute to him.

206
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:30,580
Peanut butter.

207
00:16:32,340 --> 00:16:39,740
Peanut butter as we know it in America was basically invented in 1890 by a physician in Missouri.

208
00:16:39,741 --> 00:16:47,300
and he was looking for high protein, nutritious foods to feed his patients who had trouble eating meat

209
00:16:47,301 --> 00:16:49,140
because they had poor dental health.

210
00:16:49,141 --> 00:16:55,320
The first real introduction to the masses for peanut butter happened in 1904

211
00:16:55,321 --> 00:16:59,340
at the Universal Exposition in St. Louis,

212
00:16:59,540 --> 00:17:02,980
and that's when really peanut butter was introduced to the American public.

213
00:17:03,140 --> 00:17:09,720
And then this complemented and augmented George Washington Carver's research at that time

214
00:17:09,721 --> 00:17:16,140
And the two are not unconnected because he had done laid the foundation, even though he didn't invent peanut butter.

215
00:17:16,360 --> 00:17:26,760
He had laid the foundation for raising up and digging deep into the kernel and chemically telling the world what a wonderful food product peanut was.

216
00:17:27,700 --> 00:17:32,180
His love of peanuts helped to really promote its usage widespread.

217
00:17:32,960 --> 00:17:39,400
And I think that, you know, he would be very happy to see how his legacy has really taken shape in modern day America.

218
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:48,720
We basically take a peanut that George Washington Carver helped develop and build in the field

219
00:17:48,721 --> 00:17:54,820
and help it grow to what it is today to the point that we now buy the peanut, we shell

220
00:17:54,821 --> 00:17:59,720
the peanut, and then we offer a service to customers who then further process it.

221
00:17:59,721 --> 00:18:05,920
Carver would no doubt be pleased with the assembly line efficiency of modern peanut processing

222
00:18:05,921 --> 00:18:09,918
plants like golden peanut in Dawson, Georgia.

223
00:18:09,919 --> 00:18:13,560
We sell about 25 tons an hour of peanuts.

224
00:18:14,020 --> 00:18:16,900
Takes about a truckload an hour to supply this plant.

225
00:18:19,620 --> 00:18:23,800
The peanuts that make the grade are bagged and sent to manufacturers.

226
00:18:25,740 --> 00:18:30,620
Those that don't measure up undergo a process that's come a long way since Carver's Day.

227
00:18:30,621 --> 00:18:39,200
broken kernels damaged kernels small sizes are then sent to the crushing plant there

228
00:18:39,201 --> 00:18:42,440
we process them for peanut oil and the balance being peanut meal

229
00:18:42,441 --> 00:18:52,760
when Carver produced peanut oil in his lab more than a century ago he boiled 25 pounds at a time

230
00:18:52,761 --> 00:18:56,780
